(VAR-34: reusing an already-declared name as a `For each ... in <list>` loop
 variable is claimed to reject a conflicting type. It does not — the loop
 HEADER still compiles (see D2.vox, still open). `counted` is declared a
 number, the loop rebinds it to the text elements of a list literal whose
 elements are uniformly text, and the program compiles; after the loop
 `counted` still reports Number (static) while holding a text pointer.
 RE-RECORDED 2026-08-22 against vox 0.4.9: what changed is the DOWNSTREAM
 arithmetic. It used to silently print a raw address (4198543 on the
 build that first found this, not stable across builds). vox #54 made the
 analyzer track the element type through the loop body (not the header),
 so `counted add 1` is now a compile error instead - the probe below is a
 compile-error probe now, not a runtime one.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox VAR-34.vox -o p
